WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 294: William Rufus: The Red King, the New Forest, and the Fatal Arrow of Walter Tirel

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:15.120
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series explores one of the most colorful, polarizing, and mysterious monarchs in medieval history.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.640
[Rebecca] The fierce Norman King who defied the church and met a sudden, bloody end in the new forest.

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.800
[Rebecca] Here is George with the dramatic life of William Rufus.

6
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:34.240
[George] Born around 1056 in Normandy, William was the third son of William the Conqueror and Matilda of Flanders.

7
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:47.120
[George] Unlike his rebellious, portly older brother Robert Curthos, whom their father derided as an undisciplined romantic, young William was fiercely loyal to his father, fighting at his side in every campaign.

8
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:59.040
[George] Possessing a muscular frame, yellow hair, flashing eyes, and a ruddy complexion that flushed dark crimson whenever he was enraged, he was universally dubbed Rufus, meaning the red.

9
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:10.720
[George] When the conqueror lay dying at Rouen in 1087, he disinherited Robert from England, handing Rufus a letter sealed in gold wax addressed to Archbishop Lanfranc in Canterbury.

10
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.400
[George] Riding at breakneck speed to the Channel, Rufus crossed to England and was crowned King William I.

11
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:24.880
[George] at Westminster on September 26, 1087, seizing the royal treasury at Winchester before his older brother could react.

12
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:38.800
[George] Growing up in the shadow of his elder brother Robert Curthos and younger brother Henry, Rufus was his father's favorite son precisely because he never questioned the Conqueror's commands and relished the violent hardships of military campaigning.

13
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:52.720
[George] When Robert Curthos led an armed revolt against their father in Normandy in 1079, Rufus rode into battle beside the Conqueror, taking a lance thrust to his arm in the melee and swearing eternal enmity toward his older brother.

14
00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:03.080
[George] Upon receiving his father's deathbed blessing and royal letter, Rufus galloped day and night through Normandy to Taux, sailing for England on the fastest available cutter.

15
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:09.400
[George] Knowing that the crown of England belonged to whichever son could seize the royal treasury at Winchester first.

16
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.800
[George] Rufus faced an existential crisis within months of his coronation.

17
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:24.360
[George] His powerful uncle, Bishop Odo of Bayeux, organized a massive rebellion of Norman barons who wished to reunite England and Normandy under the weak rule of Robert Curthos.

18
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.600
[George] Barricaded inside Rochester Castle, Odo was supported by the wealthiest magnates of the realm.

19
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.440
[George] Rufus demonstrated extraordinary political shrewdness.

20
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:41.560
[George] Recognizing that the Norman aristocracy was disloyal, he appealed directly to the conquered Anglo-Saxon populace.

21
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:58.760
[George] Promising lower taxes, freedom of hunting, and just laws, Rufus summoned the Saxon Fead, issuing a famous royal proclamation declaring that any freeman who failed to rally to his banner would be branded an outcast and a nithing, the ultimate Saxon insult for a coward.

22
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:08.840
[George] Thirty thousand Englishmen flocked to the Red King, storming Rochester Castle, forcing Bishop Odo into lifelong exile, and securing Rufus's throne.

23
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.960
[George] The 1088 Baronial Revolt was the greatest military peril of Rufus's life.

24
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.960
[George] His uncle Bishop Odo of Bayeu, Count Robert of Morton.

25
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:28.440
[George] And Earl Roger of Montgomery controlled the strategic fortresses of Rochester, Pevensey and Arundel, commanding private armies that outnumbered the royal household troops.

26
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:35.000
[George] Rufus's historic decision to summon the Anglo-Saxon Feud was an act of inspired populist generalship.

27
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:53.760
[George] By issuing a proclamation that branded any man who stayed home a nitting, a cowardly scoundrel devoid of honor, Rufus tapped into ancient Saxon martial pride, rallying thousands of native English yeomen who were eager to take vengeance upon the arrogant Norman barons who had oppressed them since 1066.

28
00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:00.240
[George] When Rochester surrendered, Odo was banished for life, and Rufus stood as an undisputed sovereign.

29
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.960
[George] Rufus ran his royal court with flamboyant, theatrical irreverence.

30
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:19.040
[George] His court was populated by dandyish young knights wearing pointed shoes with curled toes, long flowing hair, and lavish garments, leading pious monastic chroniclers to accuse the bachelor king of sodomy and godlessness.

31
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:26.240
[George] Rufus viewed the English church not as a spiritual authority, but as a cash cow to fund his military mercenaries.

32
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:34.800
[George] When wealthy bishoprics and abbeys fell vacant, Rufus refused to appoint successors, skimming their revenues directly into the royal treasury.

33
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:46.560
[George] In 1093, believing he was on his deathbed from a terrifying fever, a terrified Rufus appointed the saintly Italian philosopher Anselm of Beck as Archbishop of Canterbury.

34
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:54.720
[George] But once Rufus recovered, the two titans clashed furiously over royal supremacy, investiture, and church taxation.

35
00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:01.520
[George] In 1097, unable to bend the Red King's iron will, Anselm fled into exile in Rome.

36
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:12.320
[George] The deep cultural hatred between Rufus and the pious monastic chroniclers who wrote his history stemmed from the king's open contempt for clerical authority and orthodox Christian morality.

37
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:33.720
[George] Rufus openly mocked miracle tales, declaring that God would never judge an honest soldier by the dogmas of celibate priests, and amused himself by staging formal theological debates between Catholic bishops and Jewish scholars at Westminster Palace, declaring with cynical wit that if the Jews won the debate, he would convert to Judaism.

38
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:49.720
[George] When Archbishop Anselm refused to pay the exorbitant bribes Rufus demanded for his military campaigns and fled to Rome, Rufus confiscated every estate belonging to the See of Canterbury, diverting thousands of silver marks directly into his royal war chest.

39
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:56.200
[George] Rufus was an energetic and formidable military commander who pushed England's frontiers north and west.

40
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:11.880
[George] In 1092, he marched an army into Cumbria, which had long been contested by the Scottish crown, driving out the local ruler Dolphin, rebuilding the Roman fortress of Carlisle, and settling southern colonists to secure England's northern border.

41
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:23.800
[George] When his brother Robert Curthos joined the First Crusade in 1096, Rufus mortgaged the entire Duchy of Normandy for 10,000 silver marks, bringing Normandy under his direct rule.

42
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:34.280
[George] In London, Rufus constructed the colossal Westminster Hall, at 240 feet long and 67 feet wide, the largest covered hall in Western Europe.

43
00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:39.320
[George] When courtiers gasped at its staggering scale, Rufus arrogantly sneered.

44
00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:44.520
[George] This hall is half too large for a chamber, but not half large enough for a king.

45
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:56.760
[George] Rufus's military annexation of Cumbria in 1092 was a masterstroke of territorial statecraft, by expelling the Celtic chieftain Dolphin and constructing the massive stone castle of Carlisle.

46
00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:03.720
[George] Rufus pushed the northern frontier of England to the Solway Firth, where it has remained for nearly a thousand years.

47
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:15.360
[George] His acquisition of the Duchy of Normandy in 1096, when his brother Robert mortgaged the duchy to finance his crusade to Jerusalem, made Rufus the supreme master of the Anglo Norman Worman world.

48
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:31.040
[George] The monumental Westminster Hall was completed in 1099 with soaring oak hammerbeams and massive stone walls, serving as the physical monument to Rufus's imperial ambition, where he held lavish feasts for thousands of foreign ambassadors and knights.

49
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:39.360
[George] In the summer of 1100, 44-year-old Rufus was at the peak of his sovereign power, planning an expedition to seize Aquitaine.

50
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:52.000
[George] On August 1, Rufus gathered his court at his hunting lodge at Brockenhurst in the New Forest, the royal forest created by his father where dozens of Saxon villages and churches had been raised to create deer runs.

51
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.280
[George] That night, dark premonitions haunted the court.

52
00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:03.920
[George] Monks reported terrifying nightmares of the king being struck down, and Rufus himself woke sweating from a dream of blood bursting from his chest.

53
00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:10.080
[George] On the morning of August 2, an armorer presented the king with six magnificent hunting arrows.

54
00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:19.920
[George] Rufus tested their balance, kept four, and handed two to a French nobleman and expert marksman named Sir Walter Tyrol, remarking with ironic wit.

55
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:25.280
[George] It is right that the sharpest arrows should be given to him who knows how to shoot the best shots.

56
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:33.520
[George] The supernatural dread that permeated the royal hunting lodge on the eve of August 2 was recorded by every major contemporary chronicler.

57
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:46.720
[George] A Norman abbot reported a terrifying vision of Jesus Christ being whipped by the King of England, while Rufus himself suffered night terrors, crying out for lights and his chamberlains to sit beside his bed until dawn.

58
00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:58.560
[George] That morning, when a messenger arrived from Gloucester warning the king of prophetic dreams forecasting his doom, Rufus laughed loudly, declaring, Do you think that I am like an Englishman?

59
00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.880
[George] Who will abandon his purpose for the dreams of old women?

60
00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:13.560
[George] Handing the two fateful hunting arrows to Sir Walter Tyrol, Rufus led his hunting party into the dark, sweltering oak woods of the new forest, unaware that his destiny was racing to meet him.

61
00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] Late in the sweltering afternoon, as the hunting party dispersed through the dense oak woods of Canterton Glen, Rufus and Walter Tyrell dismounted near an ancient oak tree to await passing game.

62
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:31.640
[George] As the setting sun blinded the glade, a majestic red stag dashed between the king and Tyrell.

63
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:39.320
[George] Rufus fired and missed, shading his eyes with his hand and shouting, Shoot, Walter, shoot, in the devil's name.

64
00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.080
[George] Tyrol loosed his bow.

65
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:51.720
[George] The arrow struck a passing stag, or ricochet off an oak branch, and struck King William squarely in the center of his chest, burying itself to the feather in his lung and heart.

66
00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:58.600
[George] Rufus fell face down into the bracken without uttering a sound, snapping the wooden arrow shaft beneath his weight.

67
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:09.880
[George] Seeing the king lying lifeless in a pool of dark blood, Walter Tyrell panicked, leaped onto his horse, rode at a dead gallop to the coast, and fled aboard a boat to France.

68
00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:21.080
[George] The fatal moment in Canterton Glen was shrouded in the blinding late afternoon glare of the summer sun, which slanted through the thick canopy of ancient oaks and blinded the hunter's eyes.

69
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:27.240
[George] When the majestic red stag dashed through the clearing, Tyrell's arrow flew through the dappled shadows.

70
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:37.080
[George] Whether the missile glanced off an oak trunk or was aimed with treacherous precision, it struck Rufus with horrific velocity, piercing his heart and collapsing both lungs.

71
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.960
[George] The Red King did not utter a single word.

72
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:47.360
[George] He reached instinctively for the protruding wooden shaft, snapped it in half with his dying hands, and collapsed into the bracken.

73
00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:54.400
[George] His crimson blood soaking the dry soil of the forest that his father's cruelty had stolen from Saxon peasants.

74
00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:59.120
[George] The reaction to Rufus's death was cold, calculated pandemonium.

75
00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:10.240
[George] The hunting nobles, led by Rufus's younger brother Prince Henry, abandoned the dead king's body in the dirt, galloping frantically to Winchester to seize the royal treasury and the crown.

76
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:18.400
[George] Hours later, a humble local charcoal burner named Perky's discovered the blood-soaked corpse of the King of England lying in the forest.

77
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:29.440
[George] Loading the king's body onto a crude wooden two-wheeled dung cart, Perkyes drove the corpse through muddy tracks to Winchester Cathedral, blood dripping onto the ground throughout the journey.

78
00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:41.200
[George] Pious monks buried Rufus beneath the central tower of Winchester Cathedral without ringing church bells or reciting prayers, viewing his violent death as divine retribution for his impiety.

79
00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:50.000
[George] Seven years later, when the cathedral tower collapsed into rubble, locals whispered that the ground had refused to support the bones of the Red King.

80
00:11:50.000 --> 00:12:00.080
[George] William Rufus remains England's most enigmatic slain monarch, a brilliant soldier, an irreverent skeptic, and the victim of an eternal murder mystery.

81
00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:09.200
[George] The callous abandonment of the dead king's corpse by his brother Prince Henry and the royal hunting party was a chilling display of cold political opportunism.

82
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:15.600
[George] While Rufus lay bleeding in the dirt, Henry was already at Winchester demanding the keys to the royal vaults.

83
00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.960
[George] While Walter Tyrell was riding horses to death toward the coast to escape execution.

84
00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:35.240
[George] Perky's the charcoal burner, whose humble family lived in the forest for generations, loaded the royal corpse onto his rough wooden dung cart, covering it with rags as he drove into Winchester amid silence and whispers of divine judgment.

85
00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:48.520
[George] William Rufus was a king of fierce passions, magnificent military daring, and irreverent intellect, whose sudden and violent death in the New Forest cleared the throne for the glorious reign of the Lion of Justice.

86
00:12:52.520 --> 00:13:00.120
[Rebecca] King William Rufus's martial strength and mysterious death in the New Forest cleared the path for his younger brother to seize the crown.

87
00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:08.040
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with King Henry I, the tragedy of the White Ship Disaster, and the Lion of Justice.

88
00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.760
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

